IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `clpnt-dates`

-- Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1994 (NT) -- the questions of the person bringing a claim.
--
-- SEAT: the claimant: the person who brings a claim before a court of the Northern Territory or an arbitrator, where
-- the claim is governed by the law of an Australian State, another Territory or New Zealand (an injury suffered in
-- Queensland and sued on in Darwin, a contract governed by South Australian law). "Does this Act apply to my
-- proceedings?", "Is this time rule a 'limitation law'?", "Whose time limit does the court apply to my claim?", "Am I in
-- time?", "If I ask for more time, how must the court decide?". The court and the arbitrator get no seat: what they
-- would find is asked as a fact.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT. The
-- one duty with a deadline (to commence within the time the governing place's limitation law allows, which s 5 makes
-- part of the law the court applies) is a regulative rule run inside a question.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: the whole operative Act: s 3 (application), s 4 (definitions: court, limitation law), s 5
-- (characterisation of limitation laws), s 6 (exercise of discretion under limitation law). Out: s 1 (short title),
-- s 2 (commencement, used only for its date, 7 November 1994, from the endnotes' list of legislation).
--
-- Edition: Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1994 (NT), Act No. 45 of 1994, reprint REPC071 as in force at
-- 7 November 1994 (the current reprint on 6 November 2026), deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/Choice-of-Law-Limitation-Periods-Act-1994-NT-current-reprint-REPC071.txt.
-- No regulation sets a number this encoding needs.
--
-- Copyright in Northern Territory legislation is owned by the Northern Territory. Quoted from an unofficial copy under
-- the NT copyright policy of 8 October 1996 and the owner's ruling of 24 October 2026 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to a court are INPUTS ("if the courts of that
-- place would ..."). Answers follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): an answer that is
-- the text's own no is marked "(the text's no)"; an answer where the text is limited by its own wording is marked
-- "NOTHING-kind".

§ `Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1994 (NT): the questions of the person bringing a claim`

-- s 2 and the endnotes: the Act commenced on 7 November 1994 (Gaz G44, 2 November 1994, p 5). s 3 speaks of "the
-- commencement of this Act".
`the commencement of this Act` MEANS `the calendar date` 7 11 1994

-- s 4: "court includes an arbitrator". ss 5 and 6 speak of "a court of the Territory". Fork F2: an arbitrator is taken to
-- be within "a court of the Territory" when the arbitration is held in the Northern Territory.
DECLARE `Where my claim is heard` IS ONE OF
    `a court of the Northern Territory`
    `an arbitrator, in an arbitration held in the Northern Territory`
    `a court or arbitrator outside the Northern Territory`

-- ss 5 and 6: "another place being a State or another Territory of the Commonwealth or New Zealand". Which law governs a
-- claim is decided by the common law of choice of law, which the Act does not state: it is asked as a fact.
-- "another Territory of the Commonwealth": any Territory other than the Northern Territory, internal (the Australian
-- Capital Territory, the Jervis Bay Territory) or external (Norfolk Island and the others) -- fork F5.
DECLARE `The law that governs my claim` IS ONE OF
    `the law of the Northern Territory`
    `the law of an Australian State`
    `the law of another Territory of the Commonwealth`
    `the law of New Zealand`
    `the law of a country other than Australia or New Zealand`

GIVEN `the law` IS A `The law that governs my claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a State or another Territory of the Commonwealth or New Zealand` `the law` IF
        `the law` EQUALS `the law of an Australian State`
    OR  `the law` EQUALS `the law of another Territory of the Commonwealth`
    OR  `the law` EQUALS `the law of New Zealand`

GIVEN `the forum` IS A `Where my claim is heard`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a court of the Territory` `the forum` IF
        `the forum` EQUALS `a court of the Northern Territory`
    OR  `the forum` EQUALS `an arbitrator, in an arbitration held in the Northern Territory`

§§ `s 3 -- Application`

-- s 3: "This Act extends to a cause of action that arose before the commencement of this Act but does not apply to
-- proceedings instituted before that commencement."
-- The "no" is the text's no: a flat negative ("does not apply"). Unlike the Western Australian Act, this Act needs no
-- proclamation before it reaches New Zealand (finding 3).
DECLARE `Does this Act apply to my proceedings` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Act applies to these proceedings (s 3), even if the cause of action arose before 7 November 1994`
    `no: the proceedings were instituted before 7 November 1994 (s 3, the text's no)`

-- The day the proceedings were (or will be) instituted: the day given, or today if they have not been instituted yet
-- (they can be instituted no earlier than today). Fork F4.
GIVEN `instituted` IS A BOOLEAN, `instituted on` IS A DATE, `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day of institution` `instituted` `instituted on` `today` MEANS
    IF `instituted` THEN `instituted on` ELSE `today`

@export Does the Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1994 (NT) apply to my proceedings (s 3)? It applies to proceedings instituted on or after 7 November 1994, even on a cause of action that arose earlier, and not to proceedings instituted before that day.
GIVEN `instituted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already instituted proceedings on the claim (filed in court, or commenced the arbitration)
      `instituted on` IS A DATE @desc The day you instituted them (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have not yet instituted proceedings)
GIVETH A `Does this Act apply to my proceedings`
`s 3 -- does this Act apply to my proceedings` `instituted` `instituted on` `today` MEANS
    IF `the day` `falls before` `the commencement of this Act`
    THEN `no: the proceedings were instituted before 7 November 1994 (s 3, the text's no)`
    ELSE `yes: the Act applies to these proceedings (s 3), even if the cause of action arose before 7 November 1994`
    WHERE
        `the day` MEANS `the day of institution` `instituted` `instituted on` `today`

GIVEN `instituted` IS A BOOLEAN, `instituted on` IS A DATE, `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Act applies` `instituted` `instituted on` `today` IF
    `s 3 -- does this Act apply to my proceedings` `instituted` `instituted on` `today`
        EQUALS `yes: the Act applies to these proceedings (s 3), even if the cause of action arose before 7 November 1994`

§§ `s 4 -- Definitions`

-- s 4: "limitation law means a law that provides for the limitation or exclusion of any liability or the barring of a
-- right of action in respect of a claim by reference to the time when a proceeding on, or the arbitration of, the claim
-- is commenced." A "means" definition: when either element fails, the answer is the text's no (FALSE).
-- Plain gloss: a time limit for suing, whether it kills the claim, the remedy or the liability.
-- "unless the contrary intention appears": no contrary intention appears in ss 3, 5 or 6 (fork F1).
@export Is the time rule I face a "limitation law" within the Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1994 (NT) (s 4)? A limitation law is a law that limits or excludes a liability, or bars a right of action, on a claim by reference to the time when a proceeding on the claim, or its arbitration, is commenced.
GIVEN `limits or bars` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the rule limits or excludes a liability, or bars a right of action, on your claim (for example, says no action may be brought, or that the right is extinguished)
      `by the time of commencing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it does so by reference to the time when a proceeding on the claim, or the arbitration of the claim, is commenced (for example, "within 3 years after the cause of action accrued")
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 4 -- is it a limitation law` `limits or bars` `by the time of commencing` IF
        `limits or bars`
    AND `by the time of commencing`

§§ `s 5 -- Characterisation of limitation laws`

-- s 5: "If the substantive law of another place being a State or another Territory of the Commonwealth or New Zealand,
-- is to govern a claim before a court of the Territory, a limitation law of that place is to be regarded as part of that
-- substantive law and applied accordingly by the court."
-- The "if" failing is the text's no (ruling (b) of 2026-11-05): s 5 does not make the other place's limitation law
-- apply. The Act then says nothing about which time limit applies: the answer says so, and does not answer it (fork F6).
DECLARE `What s 5 says about the time limit on my claim` IS ONE OF
    `the limitation law of the place whose law governs the claim applies to it, as part of that place's substantive law (s 5)`
    `not under this Act: it does not apply to these proceedings (s 3, the text's no)`
    `s 5 does not apply: the claim is not before a court or arbitrator of the Territory (the text's no: its "if" fails); this Act does not say which time limit applies`
    `s 5 does not apply: the claim is not governed by the law of a State, another Territory or New Zealand (the text's no: its "if" fails); this Act does not say which time limit applies`

@export Whose time limit applies to my claim before a Northern Territory court or arbitrator (Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1994 (NT) s 5)? If the law of an Australian State, another Territory or New Zealand governs the claim, that place's limitation law is treated as part of its substantive law and the court applies it, not the Territory's.
GIVEN `the forum` IS A `Where my claim is heard` @desc Where your claim is (or will be) heard
      `the law` IS A `The law that governs my claim` @desc Which place's law governs your claim (its substantive law, as a court would decide under the rules of choice of law)
      `instituted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already instituted proceedings on the claim (filed in court, or commenced the arbitration)
      `instituted on` IS A DATE @desc The day you instituted them (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have not yet instituted proceedings)
GIVETH A `What s 5 says about the time limit on my claim`
`s 5 -- whose time limit applies to my claim` `the forum` `the law` `instituted` `instituted on` `today` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the Act applies` `instituted` `instituted on` `today`)
    THEN `not under this Act: it does not apply to these proceedings (s 3, the text's no)`
    ELSE IF NOT (`a court of the Territory` `the forum`)
         THEN `s 5 does not apply: the claim is not before a court or arbitrator of the Territory (the text's no: its "if" fails); this Act does not say which time limit applies`
         ELSE IF NOT (`a State or another Territory of the Commonwealth or New Zealand` `the law`)
              THEN `s 5 does not apply: the claim is not governed by the law of a State, another Territory or New Zealand (the text's no: its "if" fails); this Act does not say which time limit applies`
              ELSE `the limitation law of the place whose law governs the claim applies to it, as part of that place's substantive law (s 5)`

§§ `s 5 -- Commencing within the time that limitation law allows`

-- The claimant's duty with a deadline. The Act imposes no time limit of its own; s 5 makes the governing place's
-- limitation law part of the law the court applies, so the claimant must commence within the time that law allows. The
-- deadline is counted in days from the day time began to run under that law to the last day that law allows; both days
-- are the claimant's facts, read from that law (its counting rules are not this Act's: fork F3).
DECLARE `Claimant` IS ONE OF `the claimant`
DECLARE `Claim step` IS ONE OF `commence the proceeding or arbitration`

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claimant` `Claim step`
`s 5 -- commence within the time the governing limitation law allows` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the claimant`
    MUST `commence the proceeding or arbitration`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the claimant` BECAUSE "not commenced by the last day the limitation law of the governing place allows: s 5 makes that law part of the law the court applies"

-- Runs the rule over what happened. FULFILLED means in time and anything else late. It takes no DEONTIC from outside
-- and is not exported.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 5 -- commencing on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 5 -- commence within the time the governing limitation law allows` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the claimant` `commence the proceeding or arbitration` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Am I in time` IS ONE OF
    `in time: you commenced on or before the last day the limitation law of the governing place allows (s 5)`
    `still in time: you have not commenced, and the last day the limitation law of the governing place allows has not passed (s 5)`
    `out of time under the limitation law of the governing place (s 5): only a discretion that law gives the court can help (see s 6)`
    `this Act does not make another place's time limit apply on these facts (s 3 or s 5, the text's no)`

@export Am I in time under the limitation law that the Northern Territory court must apply to my claim (Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1994 (NT) s 5)? Where s 5 applies, you must commence the proceeding or arbitration by the last day the limitation law of the governing place allows.
GIVEN `the forum` IS A `Where my claim is heard` @desc Where your claim is (or will be) heard
      `the law` IS A `The law that governs my claim` @desc Which place's law governs your claim (its substantive law, as a court would decide under the rules of choice of law)
      `instituted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already instituted proceedings on the claim (filed in court, or commenced the arbitration)
      `instituted on` IS A DATE @desc The day you instituted them (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have not yet instituted proceedings)
      `time began on` IS A DATE @desc The day time began to run against you under the governing place's limitation law (usually when the cause of action accrued)
      `last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day on which that limitation law lets you commence the proceeding or arbitration
GIVETH A `Am I in time`
`s 5 -- am I in time` `the forum` `the law` `instituted` `instituted on` `today` `time began on` `last day` MEANS
    IF NOT (`s 5 -- whose time limit applies to my claim` `the forum` `the law` `instituted` `instituted on` `today`
                EQUALS `the limitation law of the place whose law governs the claim applies to it, as part of that place's substantive law (s 5)`)
    THEN `this Act does not make another place's time limit apply on these facts (s 3 or s 5, the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `instituted`
         THEN IF `s 5 -- commencing on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day commenced`
              THEN `in time: you commenced on or before the last day the limitation law of the governing place allows (s 5)`
              ELSE `out of time under the limitation law of the governing place (s 5): only a discretion that law gives the court can help (see s 6)`
         ELSE IF `last day` `falls before` `today`
              THEN `out of time under the limitation law of the governing place (s 5): only a discretion that law gives the court can help (see s 6)`
              ELSE `still in time: you have not commenced, and the last day the limitation law of the governing place allows has not passed (s 5)`
    WHERE
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the days from` `time began on` `to` `last day`
        `the raw day` MEANS `the days from` `time began on` `to` `instituted on`
        -- A proceeding commenced before time began to run is counted as commenced on day 0 (in time). Fork F7.
        `the day commenced` MEANS IF `the raw day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the raw day`

§§ `s 6 -- Exercise of discretion under limitation law`

-- s 6: "Where a court of the Territory exercises a discretion conferred under a limitation law of a place being a State
-- or another Territory of the Commonwealth or New Zealand, that discretion, as far as practicable, is to be exercised in
-- the manner in which it is exercised in comparable cases by the courts of that place."
-- The "where" failing is the text's no (ruling (b) of 2026-11-05). "As far as practicable" is a limit in the section's
-- own wording: where following that place's courts is not practicable, s 6 says no more (NOTHING-kind, fork F8). How the
-- courts of that place exercise the discretion in comparable cases is a judgment, asked as a fact.
DECLARE `How the court must use a discretion under that limitation law` IS ONE OF
    `as the courts of that place do in comparable cases, and on these facts they would exercise it in your favour (s 6)`
    `as the courts of that place do in comparable cases, and on these facts they would not exercise it in your favour (s 6)`
    `s 6 asks the court to follow that place's courts only as far as practicable; here it is not practicable, and s 6 says no more (NOTHING-kind)`
    `s 6 does not arise: that limitation law gives the court no discretion that you have asked it to exercise (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    `s 6 does not arise: not a court or arbitrator of the Territory, or not the limitation law of a State, another Territory or New Zealand (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    `s 6 does not arise under this Act: it does not apply to these proceedings (s 3, the text's no)`

@export If I ask the Northern Territory court to use a discretion under another place's limitation law (for example, to extend time), how must it decide (Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1994 (NT) s 6)? As far as practicable, the court exercises the discretion as the courts of that State, Territory or New Zealand exercise it in comparable cases.
GIVEN `the forum` IS A `Where my claim is heard` @desc Where your claim is (or will be) heard
      `the law` IS A `The law that governs my claim` @desc The place whose limitation law gives the discretion (usually the place whose law governs your claim)
      `instituted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already instituted proceedings on the claim (filed in court, or commenced the arbitration)
      `instituted on` IS A DATE @desc The day you instituted them (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have not yet instituted proceedings)
      `a discretion asked for` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that place's limitation law gives the court a discretion (for example, to extend the limitation period) and you have asked the court to exercise it
      `practicable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is practicable for the court to follow how the courts of that place exercise the discretion (for example, whether there are comparable cases from those courts to follow)
      `that place's courts would favour me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the courts of that place, in cases comparable to yours, would exercise the discretion in your favour
GIVETH A `How the court must use a discretion under that limitation law`
`s 6 -- how must the court use a discretion under that limitation law` `the forum` `the law` `instituted` `instituted on` `today` `a discretion asked for` `practicable` `that place's courts would favour me` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the Act applies` `instituted` `instituted on` `today`)
    THEN `s 6 does not arise under this Act: it does not apply to these proceedings (s 3, the text's no)`
    ELSE IF (NOT (`a court of the Territory` `the forum`)) OR (NOT (`a State or another Territory of the Commonwealth or New Zealand` `the law`))
         THEN `s 6 does not arise: not a court or arbitrator of the Territory, or not the limitation law of a State, another Territory or New Zealand (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
         ELSE IF NOT `a discretion asked for`
              THEN `s 6 does not arise: that limitation law gives the court no discretion that you have asked it to exercise (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
              ELSE IF NOT `practicable`
                   THEN `s 6 asks the court to follow that place's courts only as far as practicable; here it is not practicable, and s 6 says no more (NOTHING-kind)`
                   ELSE IF `that place's courts would favour me`
                        THEN `as the courts of that place do in comparable cases, and on these facts they would exercise it in your favour (s 6)`
                        ELSE `as the courts of that place do in comparable cases, and on these facts they would not exercise it in your favour (s 6)`
